Written to `/home/lqcd/wdetmold/fft/bench/gen/results/gen_r2/VERDICT.md`.

**Two corrections to the brief first, both material.** The geometry list `L = 6, 8, 17, 36` is the *ice* campaign's; this round's frozen suite is eleven cells (10, 12, 15, 20, 25, 27, 31, 32, 40, 50, 100) per `cases.txt`. And `environment.txt` says the node is a Xeon Gold **6326** — Ice Lake-SP, 1.25 MB L2, full-clock AVX-512 — not a Cascade Lake 5218. Every implementer record states its numbers were taken on **a80n0 itself** via `tryout.sh`. So there is no machine gap to attribute divergence to, and §4 of the verdict treats claim-vs-measured as a node-contention story instead. MKL moved <1% between rounds at every cell, which is what makes the r1→r2 deltas trustworthy.

**Headlines.** Panel beats the best library at all eleven cells: 3.9–4.0× at L=10/12, 3.7×/3.4× at 15/20, 3.4×/3.2× at 25/27, **8.2× at L=31**, 2.95× at 32, and 2.01×/2.00×/1.58× at 40/50/100. The margin curve is the story — it collapses to 2× exactly where the working set leaves L2. The scored suite has only one non-batched cell (L=100, `gen_pfa_large` 4947.5 µs vs MKL 7796.6); for L=10–50 I reported implementer B=1 numbers as unscored, and they expose the one place a library still wins: the SoA-8 specialists lose 2.1–2.5× to MKL at B=1 while their own generic engines overtake them there.

**Adversarial pass.** Nothing failed correctness — worst gate margin is 8× inside tolerance, worst chain value 526× inside. No build errors; two `-Waggressive-loop-optimizations` warnings in `gen_dense_prime.c:1116` and `gen_rader.c:696` (the L=31 winner and runner-up), benign — a ≤7-iteration tail loop — but named, and traced: it's the same warning gen_r1 raised against `gen_pfa_small.c:324`, which travelled into `gen_rader` with the map codelet it adopted. The only `failures.txt` entries are three `baseline_matrix` L=100 timeouts, the O(L⁴) reference floor, reproducing r1. All 55 declines cross-check exactly against `PANEL_BRIEF.md`'s acceptance table — nothing missing, and `gen_dense_prime` ran four cells it didn't owe. Zero regressions outside spread. The one real compliance failure is `gen_pfa_large`: it wins the three largest sizes while paying 0.49/0.66/**6.34 s** of plan time on every process because it never adopted the wisdom cache, where `gen_powp` pays 0.006 s.

**§4.6 is the question this round moved** — model vs search for the schedule, and it now has a number: search buys 4–16% where the model mis-ranks (L=10, 32, 100) and ~0% where it doesn't (12, 31), for 2–5 ms of warm plan time. Not §06's feared 2×, not §01's nothing. `gen_race` and `gen_planner` finishing within 0.02–1.5% at all eleven cells is the independent confirmation. §4.5 also moved (4 K aliasing and alignment worth 5–9% at a *prime* L, measured twice independently). §4.3's re-opened L2↔DRAM case — tile the batch to L2, fuse all three axes inside — was attempted by nobody, and it's the top recommendation for L=40/50/100.

`gen_bluestein` is the only entry I declined: mandated fallback, last or next-to-last at ten of eleven cells, satisfies no curation ground, and its one interesting result (Bluestein costs 3.2–4.9×, not the corpus's 107–1315×) lives in its record, which is tracked anyway.

